## Implementation Plan: `create-tutors-course`

### 1. Technology Version Decisions

The requirements specified Ink v5, but the ecosystem has evolved. Here is the recommended stack with rationale:

**Recommended: Ink 5.x + React 18 + `@inkjs/ui` 2.x**

- Ink 5.2.1 requires React >= 18.0.0 and Node >= 18 (broad compatibility)
- `@inkjs/ui` 2.0.0 declares `ink >= 5` as its peer dependency, giving us production-ready TextInput, Select, ConfirmInput, Spinner, and StatusMessage components out of the box
- `ink-text-input` 6.x and `ink-select-input` 6.x also declare `ink >= 5` and `react >= 18`
- Ink 7.x exists (v7.1.0) but requires Node 22+ and React 19.2+, which would limit the tool's audience for non-technical users who may not have Node 22

**Do NOT use `@inkjs/ui` and the standalone `ink-text-input`/`ink-select-input` together** -- `@inkjs/ui` bundles its own TextInput and Select with better theming and autocomplete. Use `@inkjs/ui` exclusively for form components.

### 2. Project Structure

```
create-tutors-course/
  package.json
  tsconfig.json
  tsup.config.ts
  README.md
  LICENSE
  src/
    cli.tsx                    # Entry point with shebang, argument parsing
    app.tsx                    # Root <App /> component managing wizard flow
    state/
      wizard-state.ts          # TypeScript types + React context for wizard state
    steps/
      step-course-details.tsx  # Step 1: Course title, description, credits
      step-topic-planning.tsx  # Step 2: Number of topics, naming each
      step-topic-content.tsx   # Step 3: Per-topic content configuration
      step-review.tsx          # Step 4: Review summary + confirm
      step-generate.tsx        # Step 5: Generation with progress feedback
    components/
      wizard-chrome.tsx        # Shared layout: header, step indicator, footer
      labeled-input.tsx        # Label + TextInput wrapper
      number-input.tsx         # Numeric input with validation
      step-indicator.tsx       # "Step 2 of 5" bar with visual progress
    generator/
      generate-course.ts       # Orchestrator: takes WizardState, creates files
      templates.ts             # Content templates for .md, .yaml files
      placeholder-image.ts     # Generates minimal PNG placeholder buffers
    utils/
      slugify.ts               # Convert user text to folder-safe slugs
      validation.ts            # Input validation helpers
```

### 3. `package.json` Shape

```json
{
  "name": "create-tutors-course",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "Interactive wizard to scaffold a tutors-sdk course folder structure",
  "type": "module",
  "bin": {
    "create-tutors-course": "./dist/cli.js"
  },
  "main": "./dist/cli.js",
  "files": ["dist"],
  "engines": {
    "node": ">=18"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsup",
    "dev": "tsup --watch",
    "prepublishOnly": "npm run build"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "ink": "^5.2.1",
    "react": "^18.3.1",
    "@inkjs/ui": "^2.0.0",
    "js-yaml": "^4.1.0",
    "pngjs": "^7.0.0"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@types/react": "^18.3.0",
    "@types/js-yaml": "^4.0.9",
    "typescript": "^5.5.0",
    "tsup": "^8.5.0",
    "@sindresorhus/tsconfig": "^3.0.1"
  },
  "keywords": ["tutors", "tutors-sdk", "course", "scaffold", "cli", "wizard"]
}
```

Key details:
- The `bin` field maps the command name `create-tutors-course` to the compiled entry point. npm's `npx` mechanism resolves `create-*` packages automatically, so `npx create-tutors-course` will install and run it.
- `"type": "module"` for ESM output.
- `"files": ["dist"]` ensures only the compiled output ships to npm.
- `pngjs` is a pure JavaScript PNG encoder/decoder with zero native dependencies -- critical for cross-platform support. It avoids the installation problems that `sharp` or `canvas` would cause for non-technical users.

### 4. `tsconfig.json`

```json
{
  "extends": "@sindresorhus/tsconfig",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "outDir": "dist",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "target": "es2022"
  },
  "include": ["src"]
}
```

### 5. `tsup.config.ts`

```ts
import { defineConfig } from "tsup";

export default defineConfig({
  entry: ["src/cli.tsx"],
  format: ["esm"],
  target: "node18",
  clean: true,
  banner: {
    js: "#!/usr/bin/env node",
  },
  // Bundle everything into a single file for npx performance
  noExternal: [/.*/],
  // Except these Node built-ins
  external: ["fs", "path", "os", "zlib", "stream", "buffer", "util", "events"],
});
```

**Important design decision**: Using `banner` to inject the shebang line rather than putting it in the source file. This avoids TypeScript/linter complaints about the shebang. Using `noExternal: [/.*/]` bundles all dependencies into a single file, which dramatically improves `npx` cold-start time (fewer files to extract from the tarball). The Node built-in modules are marked `external` since they are always available.

### 6. Wizard State Management

**File: `src/state/wizard-state.ts`**

The entire wizard state is modeled as a single TypeScript type and managed via `React.useReducer` in the root `<App>` component. This is passed down via React context.

```ts
// Content item types matching tutors-sdk folder prefixes
type ContentType = "talk" | "lab" | "note" | "web" | "github";

interface ContentItem {
  type: ContentType;
  title: string;
  // Only for 'lab' type
  stepCount?: number;
  // Only for 'web' type
  url?: string;
  // Only for 'github' type
  repoId?: string;
}

interface UnitConfig {
  title: string;
  items: ContentItem[];
}

interface TopicConfig {
  title: string;
  units: UnitConfig[];
}

interface WizardState {
  // Step 1
  courseTitle: string;
  courseDescription: string;
  credits: string;
  authLevel: 0 | 1 | 2;  // 0 = none, 1 = wall, 2 = auth required

  // Step 2
  topics: TopicConfig[];

  // Meta
  currentStep: number;         // 1-5
  outputDirectory: string;     // defaults to cwd
  generationProgress: number;  // 0-100 during step 5
  generationComplete: boolean;
}
```

**Reducer actions** (discriminated union):

```ts
type WizardAction =
  | { type: "SET_COURSE_DETAILS"; payload: Pick<WizardState, "courseTitle" | "courseDescription" | "credits" | "authLevel"> }
  | { type: "SET_TOPICS"; payload: TopicConfig[] }
  | { type: "UPDATE_TOPIC"; index: number; payload: Partial<TopicConfig> }
  | { type: "NEXT_STEP" }
  | { type: "PREV_STEP" }
  | { type: "SET_PROGRESS"; payload: number }
  | { type: "SET_COMPLETE" };
```

A `WizardContext` (`React.createContext`) exposes both `state` and `dispatch` so any step component can read and mutate the shared state.

### 7. Detailed Wizard Flow

#### Step 1: Course Details (`step-course-details.tsx`)

**What it collects:**
- Course title (required, TextInput with `onSubmit` to advance to next field)
- Course description (required, TextInput)
- Credits / author info (optional, TextInput)
- Auth level (Select with options: "None", "Login wall", "Full authentication")

**UI pattern:** Sequential field focus. Each field is shown with a label. After Enter on one field, focus advances to the next. Already-completed fields show as dimmed text above. The `@inkjs/ui` TextInput's `onSubmit` callback triggers focus advancement.

**Validation:** Course title must be non-empty (at least 3 characters). Description must be non-empty.

**Navigation:** Press Enter on the last field to advance to Step 2. A "Back" option is not needed for Step 1 since it is the first step.

#### Step 2: Topic Planning (`step-topic-planning.tsx`)

**Sub-flow:**
1. Ask "How many topics?" using a number input (1-20 range, default 3)
2. For each topic number, show a TextInput to name it ("Topic 1 title:", "Topic 2 title:", etc.)
3. After all topics are named, show the list for review

**UI pattern:** After the user enters the topic count, the screen renders N sequential TextInput fields. Each gets focus in turn. Already-named topics display as a numbered list above with green checkmarks (using `@inkjs/ui` Badge or StatusMessage).

**Data generated:** Creates the `topics` array in wizard state with the given titles and empty `units` arrays.

#### Step 3: Topic Content Configuration (`step-topic-content.tsx`)

This is the most complex step. It loops through each topic and asks the user to configure its content.

**Sub-flow per topic:**
1. Display "Configuring Topic N: [title]" as a header
2. Ask "How many units?" (number input, 1-10, default 1)
3. For each unit:
   a. Ask unit title (TextInput)
   b. Ask "How many talks?" (number, 0-10, default 1)
   c. For each talk: ask title (TextInput)
   d. Ask "How many labs?" (number, 0-5, default 0)
   e. For each lab: ask title (TextInput), ask how many steps (number, 2-20, default 3)
   f. Ask "How many notes?" (number, 0-10, default 0)
   g. For each note: ask title (TextInput)
4. After configuring all units for the topic, advance to the next topic

**Important design decision for UX:** This step has many sub-fields. To avoid overwhelming non-technical users, use "sensible defaults" aggressively. The number inputs should show the default and allow Enter to accept it. Show a running summary of configured content (e.g., "2 talks, 1 lab (3 steps), 1 note") as items are completed.

**Simplification for MVP:** For the first version, omit `web-*` and `github-*` content types from the wizard. These require URLs/repo IDs that a course author setting up for the first time is unlikely to have ready. They can be added manually to the folder structure later. This keeps the wizard fast and approachable.

#### Step 4: Review Summary (`step-review.tsx`)

**Display:**
- Course title and description
- For each topic:
  - Topic title
  - For each unit:
    - Unit title
    - Content items listed with icons/labels (e.g., "Talk: Introduction to React", "Lab: Setup Environment (3 steps)")
- Total counts: N topics, M units, X talks, Y labs, Z notes
- Output directory path

**Interaction:**
- `@inkjs/ui` ConfirmInput: "Generate this course structure? (Y/n)"
- On confirm: advance to Step 5
- On cancel: go back to Step 1 (or offer select: "Edit course details", "Edit topics", "Edit content", "Cancel")

#### Step 5: Generation (`step-generate.tsx`)

**Display:**
- `@inkjs/ui` Spinner with label showing current operation
- Progress indication using file count ("Creating file 12 of 47...")
- As each major item completes, show it with a green checkmark using `@inkjs/ui` StatusMessage
- On completion: show full summary with the path and instructions to run `npx tutors`

**Operations (see Section 8 below).**

### 8. Folder Generation Module

**File: `src/generator/generate-course.ts`**

This is the pure logic module that takes a `WizardState` object and creates the file tree. It is completely decoupled from the UI -- it could be called programmatically without Ink.

**Architecture:** The generator is a single async function that yields progress events:

```ts
interface GenerationEvent {
  type: "file" | "directory";
  path: string;
  current: number;
  total: number;
}

async function* generateCourse(
  state: WizardState,
  outputDir: string
): AsyncGenerator<GenerationEvent> {
  // ...yields events as it creates files
}
```

Using an async generator lets the UI component (`step-generate.tsx`) consume events one at a time and update the Spinner label and progress counter in real-time.

**Generation sequence:**

1. Create root directory: `<slugify(courseTitle)>/`
2. Write `course.md`:
   ```
   # {courseTitle}
   {courseDescription}
   ```
3. Write `course.png`: placeholder image (see Section 9)
4. Write `properties.yaml`:
   ```yaml
   credits: {credits}
   auth: {authLevel}
   ```
5. For each topic (index `i`, 0-based):
   - Create `topic-{padded(i+1)}-{slugify(topicTitle)}/`
   - Write `topic.md`:
     ```
     # {topicTitle}
     {topicTitle} content
     ```
   - Write `topic.png`: placeholder image
   - For each unit (index `j`, 0-based):
     - Create `unit-{j+1}/`
     - Write `unit.md` (just the unit title, no heading -- matching the reference course convention)
     - For each talk (index `k`, 0-based):
       - Create `talk-{k+1}-{slugify(talkTitle)}/`
       - Write `{slugify(talkTitle)}.md`:
         ```
         # {talkTitle}
         A short summary of the talk.
         ```
       - Write `{slugify(talkTitle)}.pdf`: minimal valid PDF placeholder
       - Write `{slugify(talkTitle)}.png`: placeholder image
     - For each lab (index `k`, 0-based):
       - Create `book-{letter(k)}/` (using a, b, c, etc.)
       - Write `00.{slugify(labTitle)}.md`:
         ```
         # Objectives
         {labTitle} objectives.
         ```
       - For each step (1 to stepCount):
         - Write `{padded(step)}.Step-{padded(step)}.md`:
           ```
           # Step {step}
           Step {step} content.
           ```
       - Create `img/`
       - Write `img/main.png`: placeholder image
     - For each note (index `k`, 0-based):
       - Create `note-{k+1}/`
       - Write `note.md`:
         ```
         # {noteTitle}
         {noteTitle} content.
         ```
       - Write `note.png`: placeholder image

**Key conventions observed from the reference course:**

- Topic folder: `topic-{NN}-{slug}/` with zero-padded two-digit numbers
- Topic metadata: `topic.md` and `topic.png` (the reference course uses both `topic.md` and `{name}.md` -- the generator should use `topic.md` for simplicity)
- Unit folder: `unit-{N}/` (single digit, 1-based)
- Unit metadata: a single `.md` file containing just the title text (no `#` heading), named descriptively (e.g., `main-lesson.md`). For simplicity, the generator will use `unit.md`.
- Talk folder: `talk-{N}-{slug}/` containing `{name}.md`, `{name}.pdf`, `{name}.png`
- Book/lab folder: `book-{letter}/` containing numbered step files like `00.Lab-Title.md`, `01.Step-01.md`
- Note folder: `note-{N}/` containing `note.md` and `note.png`
- The `weburl` file for web types contains just the bare URL
- The `githubid` file for github types contains a GitHub user/repo string

### 9. Placeholder Image Generation

**File: `src/generator/placeholder-image.ts`**

Using `pngjs` (pure JavaScript, zero native dependencies), generate solid-color PNG images:

```ts
import { PNG } from "pngjs";

function createPlaceholderPng(
  width: number,
  height: number,
  color: { r: number; g: number; b: number }
): Buffer {
  const png = new PNG({ width, height });
  for (let y = 0; y < height; y++) {
    for (let x = 0; x < width; x++) {
      const idx = (width * y + x) << 2;
      png.data[idx] = color.r;
      png.data[idx + 1] = color.g;
      png.data[idx + 2] = color.b;
      png.data[idx + 3] = 255; // alpha
    }
  }
  return PNG.sync.write(png);
}
```

Use different soft colors for different content types:
- Course: `{ r: 100, g: 149, b: 237 }` (cornflower blue)
- Topic: `{ r: 144, g: 238, b: 144 }` (light green)
- Talk: `{ r: 255, g: 218, b: 185 }` (peach)
- Lab/book: `{ r: 221, g: 160, b: 221 }` (plum)
- Note: `{ r: 255, g: 255, b: 224 }` (light yellow)

Image dimensions: 300x170 pixels (a reasonable card image size that the tutors reader will display well).

**Placeholder PDF generation:** Embed a minimal valid PDF as a Buffer constant. A valid PDF can be as small as ~100 bytes. The generator writes this buffer to `{name}.pdf` for each talk.

```ts
const PLACEHOLDER_PDF = Buffer.from(
  "%PDF-1.0\n1 0 obj<</Type/Catalog/Pages 2 0 R>>endobj\n" +
  "2 0 obj<</Type/Pages/Kids[3 0 R]/Count 1>>endobj\n" +
  "3 0 obj<</Type/Page/MediaBox[0 0 612 792]/Parent 2 0 R>>endobj\n" +
  "xref\n0 4\n0000000000 65535 f \n0000000009 00000 n \n" +
  "0000000058 00000 n \n0000000115 00000 n \n" +
  "trailer<</Size 4/Root 1 0 R>>\nstartxref\n190\n%%EOF"
);
```

### 10. Key Ink Components

**File: `src/components/wizard-chrome.tsx`**

